WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.323 --> 00:00:04.463
- Then along comes this
young guy out of Oakland.

00:00:04.463 --> 00:00:05.723
I'm going, "What?"

00:00:05.723 --> 00:00:06.619
- Ryan Coogler.

00:00:06.619 --> 00:00:07.592
- Ryan Coogler.

00:00:07.592 --> 00:00:08.721
- There's a short sightedness that

00:00:08.721 --> 00:00:11.306
comes with playing sports.

00:00:11.306 --> 00:00:12.639
I don't even know if college has a dean

00:00:12.639 --> 00:00:13.847
of discipline but my high school did.

00:00:13.847 --> 00:00:15.680
It's like the dean of discipline
at the school and it's her,

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:17.557
and I'm going for this horrible meeting.

00:00:17.557 --> 00:00:21.559
I was shocked, I was angered,
I was frustrated, scared.

00:00:21.559 --> 00:00:22.866
There's nothing like seeing a great

00:00:22.866 --> 00:00:25.820
movie and being a filmmaker
yourself 'cause it's inspiring.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:28.517
- He's an American film
director and screen writer.

00:00:28.517 --> 00:00:30.686
His first feature film, Fruitvale Station,

00:00:30.686 --> 00:00:34.258
won two awards at the 2013
Sundance Film Festival.

00:00:34.258 --> 00:00:35.935
He also wrote and directed the seventh

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:38.421
film in the Rocky saga, Creed.

00:00:38.421 --> 00:00:40.177
He's Ryan Coogler and here is my take

00:00:40.177 --> 00:00:41.837
on his top 10 rules of success.

00:00:41.837 --> 00:00:43.484
Rule number one is my personal favorite

00:00:43.484 --> 00:00:44.739
and I'm really curious to figure out

00:00:44.739 --> 00:00:46.391
which one you guys like the best.

00:00:46.391 --> 00:00:47.951
Also, as Ryan is talking, if he says

00:00:47.951 --> 00:00:49.552
something that's really impactful to you

00:00:49.552 --> 00:00:51.094
that really means a lot to you,

00:00:51.094 --> 00:00:52.528
please leave it in the comments below

00:00:52.528 --> 00:00:54.193
and put quotes around it so other

00:00:54.193 --> 00:00:56.120
people can be inspired as well.

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:56.953
Enjoy.

00:00:56.953 --> 00:01:00.016
(whooshing and booming)

00:01:00.016 --> 00:01:02.849
(uplifting music)

00:01:09.956 --> 00:01:14.209
- The last Rocky movie before
Creed, the final scene.

00:01:14.209 --> 00:01:16.786
Was it your way of saying goodbye

00:01:16.786 --> 00:01:18.954
to the character, goodbye to that?

00:01:18.954 --> 00:01:20.070
- Yeah, goodbye to everything.

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:22.877
Goodbye to the best chapters of my life,

00:01:22.877 --> 00:01:25.022
at least professional life, it really was.

00:01:25.022 --> 00:01:29.364
Then along comes this
young guy out of Oakland.

00:01:29.364 --> 00:01:30.620
I'm going, "What?"

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:31.640
- Ryan Coogler.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:32.716
- Ryan Coogler.

00:01:32.716 --> 00:01:34.756
- [Narrator] When director
Ryan Coogler first met

00:01:34.756 --> 00:01:38.193
with Stallone, he was untested
as a feature filmmaker.

00:01:38.193 --> 00:01:40.284
But he had an intensely personal idea

00:01:40.284 --> 00:01:42.629
for reviving the Rocky franchise.

00:01:42.629 --> 00:01:46.352
Coogler's father had fallen
gravely ill a few years earlier.

00:01:46.352 --> 00:01:48.475
With Ryan by his side, there was only

00:01:48.475 --> 00:01:51.807
one thing the elder
Coogler wanted to watch.

00:01:51.807 --> 00:01:55.116
- The father's a very soulful powerful man

00:01:55.116 --> 00:01:58.565
and now he's being reduced to
a shell of his former self.

00:01:58.565 --> 00:02:03.289
But oddly, he watches Rocky
again, and again, and again.

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:05.791
His son, who loves his father,
sitting right beside him,

00:02:05.791 --> 00:02:08.631
he's watching his father
disintegrate before his very eyes.

00:02:08.631 --> 00:02:11.431
The traumatic impact was so imprinted

00:02:11.431 --> 00:02:14.813
on this young man that
he never got over it.

00:02:14.813 --> 00:02:16.855
He comes to me, he goes, "Hey,
well I got this great idea.

00:02:16.855 --> 00:02:18.948
"We're going to revive Rocky."

00:02:18.948 --> 00:02:19.964
I go, "How?"

00:02:19.964 --> 00:02:21.074
When he told me the story, I said,

00:02:21.074 --> 00:02:23.656
"This is insane, wrong.

00:02:23.656 --> 00:02:26.622
"You're a young kid, you
haven't done anything yet.

00:02:26.622 --> 00:02:28.471
"But I appreciate the thought."

00:02:28.471 --> 00:02:29.947
He goes, "Okay, see you around."

00:02:29.947 --> 00:02:32.302
He goes out and he does a
masterpiece, Fruitvale Station,

00:02:32.302 --> 00:02:33.833
wins the Cannes Film Festival.

00:02:33.833 --> 00:02:34.975
Every studio wants him.

00:02:34.975 --> 00:02:35.848
What's he want to do?

00:02:35.848 --> 00:02:36.681
Creed.

00:02:36.681 --> 00:02:39.706
I went now either this guy here is just

00:02:39.706 --> 00:02:43.873
trying my patience or he reminds
me of a guy I used to know.

00:02:45.196 --> 00:02:48.586
- I was going to say that
was you 40 years ago.

00:02:48.586 --> 00:02:51.329
- Yeah, it was, absolutely.

00:02:51.329 --> 00:02:53.889
Then I realized this
is not a movie for him.

00:02:53.889 --> 00:02:55.874
It's for his father.

00:02:55.874 --> 00:02:59.308
This is all about a love
letter to his father.

00:02:59.308 --> 00:03:02.260
- If you really dug down deep
and asked a real Rocky fan

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:04.347
why he likes the movies, it's
usually because they watched

00:03:04.347 --> 00:03:06.032
them with somebody that they love.

00:03:06.032 --> 00:03:08.845
What's so great about them is
they're so personal to people.

00:03:08.845 --> 00:03:10.791
People just associate
it with their family.

00:03:10.791 --> 00:03:12.149
The first time I told my dad that

00:03:12.149 --> 00:03:13.263
I was going to go to film school,

00:03:13.263 --> 00:03:16.791
and not continue to play
football, he was heartbroken.

00:03:16.791 --> 00:03:17.776
He was really upset.

00:03:17.776 --> 00:03:19.583
He didn't want me to do it.

00:03:19.583 --> 00:03:22.455
I thought that was going to be what I was.

00:03:22.455 --> 00:03:25.038
But there's a short sightedness

00:03:25.912 --> 00:03:29.245
that comes with play sports for so long.

00:03:32.204 --> 00:03:34.774
The shelf life on a career in athletics

00:03:34.774 --> 00:03:38.941
is much more compressed than
a career in other things.

00:03:40.066 --> 00:03:43.165
I kind of thought about this idea.

00:03:43.165 --> 00:03:44.569
You're playing football,
if you're fortunate

00:03:44.569 --> 00:03:46.298
enough to keep playing professionally,

00:03:46.298 --> 00:03:49.060
maybe until about 30,
you're considered a veteran.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:50.945
You know what I mean?

00:03:50.945 --> 00:03:52.649
I was very interested in getting

00:03:52.649 --> 00:03:55.399
into a space where I was flipped.

00:03:56.475 --> 00:03:58.149
If you tell somebody you're
a 30 year old filmmaker,

00:03:58.149 --> 00:04:00.477
they're like, "Aw, you're a baby."

00:04:00.477 --> 00:04:04.137
You got so much more time to
grow, so much time to learn

00:04:04.137 --> 00:04:06.980
more about the craft
and continue to stretch.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:10.796
You can do it until you're
a winner in your life.

00:04:10.796 --> 00:04:11.629
You know what I'm saying?

00:04:11.629 --> 00:04:16.159
That was what kind of attracted
me to switching careers.

00:04:16.159 --> 00:04:18.841
A college coach once said,

00:04:18.841 --> 00:04:23.336
"95% of college football is recruiting."

00:04:23.336 --> 00:04:25.574
If you're able to get talented players,

00:04:25.574 --> 00:04:29.074
a lot of you're work is done for yourself.

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:31.028
Same thing goes for directing.

00:04:31.028 --> 00:04:33.496
When you cast the right actors,

00:04:33.496 --> 00:04:36.081
you find talented actors
that are generally

00:04:36.081 --> 00:04:38.837
great people to spend
time with and be around.

00:04:38.837 --> 00:04:41.119
A lot of your work is done for yourself.

00:04:41.119 --> 00:04:44.786
I definitely got into
this because I believe

00:04:46.605 --> 00:04:49.858
in the power of emotion,
you know what I mean?

00:04:49.858 --> 00:04:54.325
I think that filmmaking
is an incredible way

00:04:54.325 --> 00:04:56.393
to trigger empathy in other people

00:04:56.393 --> 00:04:58.950
and do tell stories to get people

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:02.048
to relate to characters
that they never met before.

00:05:02.048 --> 00:05:05.950
I think that that is something
that I'll always want to be in

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:10.415
tune with, not just as an
artist but as a human being.

00:05:10.415 --> 00:05:13.098
Putting passion in the
things, into my craft,

00:05:13.098 --> 00:05:15.824
and putting emotion into my craft.

00:05:15.824 --> 00:05:18.098
It's kind of like this
creature of two worlds.

00:05:18.098 --> 00:05:20.074
I went to private school but I lived

00:05:20.074 --> 00:05:24.419
in some of the more difficult
neighborhoods in the Bay Area.

00:05:24.419 --> 00:05:25.923
So I didn't really fit in anywhere

00:05:25.923 --> 00:05:27.686
except the football field.

00:05:27.686 --> 00:05:30.213
I really like math and science.

00:05:30.213 --> 00:05:33.460
I got a football scholarship
to a liberal arts school

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:35.812
called St. Mary's College in the Bay Area.

00:05:35.812 --> 00:05:38.562
I went in there and I
wanted to major in chemistry

00:05:38.562 --> 00:05:39.940
'cause I really like chemistry.

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:41.844
I figured that if
football didn't work out,

00:05:41.844 --> 00:05:46.011
I could always go to med
school and become a doctor.

00:05:47.284 --> 00:05:48.801
That school, 'cause it
was a liberal arts school,

00:05:48.801 --> 00:05:50.892
they made you take these
creative writing classes.

00:05:50.892 --> 00:05:52.701
I took this creative
writing class taught by this

00:05:52.701 --> 00:05:56.409
professor, who is also a
novelist, named Rosemary Graham.

00:05:56.409 --> 00:05:59.201
On the first day of class,
we sit down and she just

00:05:59.201 --> 00:06:01.266
talks about how much she
hates football players.

00:06:01.266 --> 00:06:02.976
(all laughing)

00:06:02.976 --> 00:06:03.809
- [Eugene] Really?

00:06:03.809 --> 00:06:05.003
- Yeah and how football is like the most

00:06:05.003 --> 00:06:07.722
barbaric ridiculous sport,
how it should be outlawed.

00:06:07.722 --> 00:06:09.589
I'm like in the front of the class,

00:06:09.589 --> 00:06:11.153
like I think I want to leave.

00:06:11.153 --> 00:06:13.415
(audience laughs)

00:06:13.415 --> 00:06:16.589
But we talked, we had a little argument

00:06:16.589 --> 00:06:18.957
and we agreed to disagree at the time.

00:06:18.957 --> 00:06:22.572
She assigned us an assignment
which was tell me about your

00:06:22.572 --> 00:06:24.130
most emotional experience
you've ever had in your life.

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:27.573
I want you to write about in
prose and then turn it in.

00:06:27.573 --> 00:06:28.708
I wrote about mine.

00:06:28.708 --> 00:06:30.211
I didn't think much about it.

00:06:30.211 --> 00:06:31.669
I did it honestly.

00:06:31.669 --> 00:06:33.738
If I would've been thinking,
I probably wouldn't have

00:06:33.738 --> 00:06:36.315
written about what I wrote about.

00:06:36.315 --> 00:06:38.742
I turned it in and I was in
my dorm, hanging out with

00:06:38.742 --> 00:06:41.713
a bunch of my football
teammates, with my neighborhood,

00:06:41.713 --> 00:06:44.778
and I got a call from
her on my dorm phone.

00:06:44.778 --> 00:06:48.402
She's like, "Hey, what are you doing?"

00:06:48.402 --> 00:06:49.496
I'm like, "I'm in my dorm."

00:06:49.496 --> 00:06:50.496
She's like, "I need you to come

00:06:50.496 --> 00:06:52.610
"down to my office immediately.

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:53.991
"It's about your assignment."

00:06:53.991 --> 00:06:55.738
I'm like, Jesus Christ.

00:06:55.738 --> 00:06:56.992
(audience laughs)

00:06:56.992 --> 00:06:59.098
I'm like why did I write about that?

00:06:59.098 --> 00:07:00.288
I kicked everybody out of my dorm.

00:07:00.288 --> 00:07:02.291
I'm like, "You all got to go."

00:07:02.291 --> 00:07:05.596
Then I walked down to
her office and I'm just

00:07:05.596 --> 00:07:07.923
imagining like, I got an
overactive imagination.

00:07:07.923 --> 00:07:11.243
I'm imagining opening up the
door and there's like police.

00:07:11.243 --> 00:07:13.399
(audience laughs)

00:07:13.399 --> 00:07:16.555
(laughs) I don't even know if college

00:07:16.555 --> 00:07:18.354
has a dean of discipline
but my high school did.

00:07:18.354 --> 00:07:20.562
It's like the dean of discipline
at the school and it's her,

00:07:20.562 --> 00:07:22.493
and I'm going for this horrible meeting.

00:07:22.493 --> 00:07:25.328
I open up the door, and I
go in, and it's just her.

00:07:25.328 --> 00:07:27.568
She's like, "Sit down." (laughs)

00:07:27.568 --> 00:07:29.492
I go walk over and I sit
down across from her,

00:07:29.492 --> 00:07:32.293
like I'm sitting down
with Eugene right now.

00:07:32.293 --> 00:07:33.317
She's like looking at me.

00:07:33.317 --> 00:07:34.699
You know those uncomfortable silences?

00:07:34.699 --> 00:07:36.157
She's like looking at
me, I'm looking back.

00:07:36.157 --> 00:07:38.093
You know what I mean? (laughing)

00:07:38.093 --> 00:07:40.427
She says, "What do you want
to do when you grow up?"

00:07:40.427 --> 00:07:42.835
I'm like man, this is terrible.

00:07:42.835 --> 00:07:45.407
(audience laughs)

00:07:45.407 --> 00:07:47.933
I tell her I want to be a doctor.

00:07:47.933 --> 00:07:49.433
She's like, "Why?"

00:07:50.997 --> 00:07:52.757
Where I come from, if you tell somebody

00:07:52.757 --> 00:07:55.119
you want to be doctor when you
grow up, nobody asks you why.

00:07:55.119 --> 00:07:56.318
They're like, "Great."

00:07:56.318 --> 00:07:57.690
(audience laughs)

00:07:57.690 --> 00:08:01.661
"That's awesome." (laughs)

00:08:01.661 --> 00:08:03.260
She was like, "Why?"

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:04.343
I was like, "Nobody ever asks me,"

00:08:04.343 --> 00:08:07.022
so I'm like, huh, why do
I want to be a doctor?

00:08:07.022 --> 00:08:09.531
I was like, "Oh, I'm
good at math and science,

00:08:09.531 --> 00:08:12.921
"and I figure you should use
that if you're good at it.

00:08:12.921 --> 00:08:14.372
"What better way to use
it than to help people?"

00:08:14.372 --> 00:08:17.329
I figured doctors can make money honestly

00:08:17.329 --> 00:08:18.588
and help people while you're doing it,

00:08:18.588 --> 00:08:22.050
be a good positive
influence on the community.

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:24.800
What's wrong with being a doctor?

00:08:25.694 --> 00:08:27.609
She was like, "Hmm."

00:08:27.609 --> 00:08:30.039
Then she grabbed my
paper, and she looked it.

00:08:30.039 --> 00:08:32.034
She was like, "Well I read your paper

00:08:32.034 --> 00:08:34.210
"and it was really visual.

00:08:34.210 --> 00:08:37.273
"While I was reading it,
I felt like I was there."

00:08:37.273 --> 00:08:39.608
She said, "That's actually arguably rarer

00:08:39.608 --> 00:08:42.001
"than being good at math or science,

00:08:42.001 --> 00:08:44.062
"and you should consider being a writer.

00:08:44.062 --> 00:08:46.083
"You can do the same
things you're talking about

00:08:46.083 --> 00:08:48.582
"but maybe even on a bigger level.

00:08:48.582 --> 00:08:50.299
"You should maybe even
consider going to Hollywood

00:08:50.299 --> 00:08:53.437
"and writing screenplays,"
is what she said.

00:08:53.437 --> 00:08:56.314
I was completely weirded out
at that moment, I was like--

00:08:56.314 --> 00:08:57.805
- What did you think
when she told you that?

00:08:57.805 --> 00:08:59.001
- I thought she was crazy.

00:08:59.001 --> 00:09:00.532
(audience laughs)

00:09:00.532 --> 00:09:02.147
I didn't even know what a screenplay was.

00:09:02.147 --> 00:09:03.108
You know what I mean?

00:09:03.108 --> 00:09:04.461
I remember looking at her and she had

00:09:04.461 --> 00:09:06.126
all these books behind her.

00:09:06.126 --> 00:09:08.317
She had this wall of books.

00:09:08.317 --> 00:09:10.200
I got ADD, so I remember being

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:13.309
insanely jealous 'cause
she had so many books.

00:09:13.309 --> 00:09:15.884
I'm like well I like books,
why don't I have any books?

00:09:15.884 --> 00:09:17.953
(audience laughs)

00:09:17.953 --> 00:09:20.453
(Ryan laughs)

00:09:22.604 --> 00:09:24.604
I'll come back to that.

00:09:26.218 --> 00:09:28.968
But she said, "I think you should

00:09:31.139 --> 00:09:34.389
"think about even writing screenplays."

00:09:35.647 --> 00:09:36.756
I was thinking she was crazy.

00:09:36.756 --> 00:09:38.554
So I'm like, "Wait, so
I'm not in trouble?"

00:09:38.554 --> 00:09:40.692
She's like, "No, why would be in trouble?"

00:09:40.692 --> 00:09:41.847
I was like, "Cool."

00:09:41.847 --> 00:09:44.221
(audience laughs)

00:09:44.221 --> 00:09:46.182
I'm like I got to get out of
this crazy book lady's office.

00:09:46.182 --> 00:09:47.015
You know what I mean?

00:09:47.015 --> 00:09:49.113
I'm like, "Are we all good here?"

00:09:49.113 --> 00:09:50.151
She's like, "Yeah, that's fine.

00:09:50.151 --> 00:09:51.340
"I just wanted to talk to you about that.

00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:52.658
"You should think about it."

00:09:52.658 --> 00:09:55.673
I'm like, "Cool," so I left and
I went back to my dorm room.

00:09:55.673 --> 00:09:57.025
My dorm room was empty and I was like

00:09:57.025 --> 00:10:00.140
that was the weirdest conversation
I ever had in my life.

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:03.298
I didn't even know what a
screenplay was, I never seen one.

00:10:03.298 --> 00:10:07.270
But I remember thinking
about how much I like movies.

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:08.821
I always liked movies.

00:10:08.821 --> 00:10:10.284
I always liked to read.

00:10:10.284 --> 00:10:11.660
Then I started looking around my dorm room

00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:13.489
and all I had was like rap posters.

00:10:13.489 --> 00:10:14.482
I had no books.

00:10:14.482 --> 00:10:17.065
(all laughing)

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:19.867
I had no movies.

00:10:19.867 --> 00:10:21.965
I was like but I like
books, I like movies.

00:10:21.965 --> 00:10:25.882
Maybe I'll should start buying
books and buying movies.

00:10:25.882 --> 00:10:28.292
(audience laughs)

00:10:28.292 --> 00:10:30.333
Then I was thinking about what
she said about a screenplay.

00:10:30.333 --> 00:10:32.865
I'm like maybe I should check
out what one looks like.

00:10:32.865 --> 00:10:37.756
I had $20, I hopped in my
car, drove to Circuit City.

00:10:37.756 --> 00:10:39.908
I walked around the DVD section

00:10:39.908 --> 00:10:41.698
and I found this Pulp Fiction.

00:10:41.698 --> 00:10:43.783
I like Pulp Fiction, and I picked it up,

00:10:43.783 --> 00:10:44.999
and it was a special edition.

00:10:44.999 --> 00:10:49.905
It had a CD-ROM with the
screenplay built into it.

00:10:49.905 --> 00:10:52.383
It was like $17.99 or something
so I'm like this is perfect.

00:10:52.383 --> 00:10:54.781
I'd buy this, went back to the dorms,

00:10:54.781 --> 00:10:57.832
popped the CD-ROM in, and
saw my first screenplay.

00:10:57.832 --> 00:11:01.887
It was like, I don't know man,
it was like, I don't know.

00:11:01.887 --> 00:11:02.931
It reminded me of the first time

00:11:02.931 --> 00:11:04.767
I stepped on the football
field and hit somebody.

00:11:04.767 --> 00:11:08.526
I felt like was at home,
you know what I mean?

00:11:08.526 --> 00:11:10.786
I looked at it and I
opened up Microsoft Word,

00:11:10.786 --> 00:11:12.646
and I thought the formatting
was kind of weird.

00:11:12.646 --> 00:11:13.705
So I started trying to like ape

00:11:13.705 --> 00:11:15.827
the formatting in Microsoft Word.

00:11:15.827 --> 00:11:19.994
I started to write my first
screenplay that night.

00:11:21.869 --> 00:11:23.533
I stayed up till really
late in the morning.

00:11:23.533 --> 00:11:25.115
I started to do it every day.

00:11:25.115 --> 00:11:26.568
I'll find myself leaving the weight room,

00:11:26.568 --> 00:11:30.235
like I got to go back
and get on that script.

00:11:31.370 --> 00:11:33.849
I realized that I really loved to do it.

00:11:33.849 --> 00:11:35.506
That professor's like one of my closest

00:11:35.506 --> 00:11:36.938
friends to this day.
- Wow.

00:11:36.938 --> 00:11:38.295
- You got guys that are salt, you got guys

00:11:38.295 --> 00:11:40.387
that are good, you got
guys that are great.

00:11:40.387 --> 00:11:42.117
It's the same thing like in athletics.

00:11:42.117 --> 00:11:43.758
You know what I mean?

00:11:43.758 --> 00:11:44.716
It's the difference between

00:11:44.716 --> 00:11:45.877
a good player and a great player.

00:11:45.877 --> 00:11:48.502
A great player makes
those better around him.

00:11:48.502 --> 00:11:51.608
A great player is
somebody who is incredibly

00:11:51.608 --> 00:11:55.410
has god given talent but also a naturally

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:57.427
consistent work ethic,
you know what I mean?

00:11:57.427 --> 00:12:02.316
When those two things meet,
that's when you get greatness.

00:12:02.316 --> 00:12:05.746
I always had a backup
plan, you know what I mean?

00:12:05.746 --> 00:12:08.505
It was never really a backup plan.

00:12:08.505 --> 00:12:12.005
I imagined in finance, because I liked it.

00:12:13.492 --> 00:12:17.048
But at the same time I
was doing screenwriting.

00:12:17.048 --> 00:12:18.935
It's like when I was in film school,

00:12:18.935 --> 00:12:22.655
I still had my job back in the Bay Area.

00:12:22.655 --> 00:12:23.989
- [Interviewer] The doctor thing,

00:12:23.989 --> 00:12:25.071
you just decided not for me?

00:12:25.071 --> 00:12:26.118
- Yeah, I scrapped it.

00:12:26.118 --> 00:12:27.272
(both laughing)

00:12:27.272 --> 00:12:28.844
It was tough, like doing chemistry

00:12:28.844 --> 00:12:30.078
and playing football at the same time.

00:12:30.078 --> 00:12:31.660
It was like next to impossible.

00:12:31.660 --> 00:12:33.988
I knew I didn't want to be
an undergrad for a long time

00:12:33.988 --> 00:12:36.327
and on that track, because
of football practice,

00:12:36.327 --> 00:12:39.412
and because of labs, I would've
never graduated on time.

00:12:39.412 --> 00:12:41.465
It would've took me like six
or seven years to graduate.

00:12:41.465 --> 00:12:45.172
I got the idea for doing this movie

00:12:45.172 --> 00:12:49.136
shortly after the
incident itself happened.

00:12:49.136 --> 00:12:53.807
I was back home in the Bay
Area when Oscar was shot.

00:12:53.807 --> 00:12:57.566
I heard about it around the
same time everybody else did.

00:12:57.566 --> 00:13:00.429
You get a call from people
who were riding the train.

00:13:00.429 --> 00:13:05.187
How the Bay Area works
is, San Francisco is where

00:13:05.187 --> 00:13:07.969
everybody goes to see the
fireworks on New Year's Eve.

00:13:07.969 --> 00:13:08.979
Everybody from the East Bay going,

00:13:08.979 --> 00:13:10.236
"Hey, we're going to Frisco tonight.

00:13:10.236 --> 00:13:13.204
"We're going to see the
fireworks burst off."

00:13:13.204 --> 00:13:16.377
You go down to Embarcadero and you

00:13:16.377 --> 00:13:17.648
watch 'em behind the clock tower.

00:13:17.648 --> 00:13:19.473
So everybody in the Bay Area's there

00:13:19.473 --> 00:13:23.848
and the only way to really
get to San Francisco,

00:13:23.848 --> 00:13:26.917
to get to that spot, if you're
sane, is to catch the BART.

00:13:26.917 --> 00:13:28.563
People come from all different backgrounds

00:13:28.563 --> 00:13:30.261
and everybody's on BART that night.

00:13:30.261 --> 00:13:31.500
Everybody's coming back.

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:32.439
So when that incident happened,

00:13:32.439 --> 00:13:33.272
a lot of people heard about it.

00:13:33.272 --> 00:13:35.731
A lot of people called and
said, "Somebody got shot."

00:13:35.731 --> 00:13:37.649
The BART station, you
couldn't get back on.

00:13:37.649 --> 00:13:38.898
The train was delayed and people

00:13:38.898 --> 00:13:40.600
were saying they were on the train.

00:13:40.600 --> 00:13:43.124
I ended up hearing about it that night.

00:13:43.124 --> 00:13:47.291
I was actually working as a
bouncer at a rave in the city.

00:13:48.384 --> 00:13:49.745
I got off and I heard about it.

00:13:49.745 --> 00:13:50.740
Later on that morning, we found out

00:13:50.740 --> 00:13:53.573
that he passed away at a hospital.

00:13:54.674 --> 00:13:57.841
The Bay Area kind of was in an upheaval.

00:13:59.351 --> 00:14:01.318
It was shortly after that we saw the tape.

00:14:01.318 --> 00:14:05.856
We saw what happened to him
on YouTube and on the news.

00:14:05.856 --> 00:14:09.610
For me, I was shocked, I was angered,

00:14:09.610 --> 00:14:11.693
I was frustrated, scared.

00:14:12.925 --> 00:14:16.436
More than anything, filled with question

00:14:16.436 --> 00:14:19.157
as to how this could happen, and why

00:14:19.157 --> 00:14:21.150
stuff like this continues to happen,

00:14:21.150 --> 00:14:24.746
and how it could happen in
the Bay Area where I'm from.

00:14:24.746 --> 00:14:27.150
We thought that this,
you know what I mean?

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:30.244
Thought we were past this kind of stuff.

00:14:30.244 --> 00:14:32.584
Fortunately, if I'm a filmmaker,

00:14:32.584 --> 00:14:35.098
and as an artist, I think it's very

00:14:35.098 --> 00:14:37.173
natural for artists to get
their fears, and their angers,

00:14:37.173 --> 00:14:39.922
and their frustrations
out through their art.

00:14:39.922 --> 00:14:41.783
So any time I see something that moves me,

00:14:41.783 --> 00:14:43.396
I think about it being in a movie,

00:14:43.396 --> 00:14:46.146
or putting it out in that format.

00:14:47.156 --> 00:14:48.569
Especially after I saw what happened

00:14:48.569 --> 00:14:52.736
immediately afterwards, and
how the case was politicized,

00:14:55.881 --> 00:14:58.234
I thought that what if I was able

00:14:58.234 --> 00:15:01.079
to make a film about this character

00:15:01.079 --> 00:15:02.871
and maybe people who wouldn't be able

00:15:02.871 --> 00:15:06.511
to come around characters
like this, to see it,

00:15:06.511 --> 00:15:08.654
and have insight on what it
is to be someone like this,

00:15:08.654 --> 00:15:10.801
and have insight on why, when these

00:15:10.801 --> 00:15:12.133
things happen, they're a tragedy.

00:15:12.133 --> 00:15:14.065
It's not just something that you should

00:15:14.065 --> 00:15:16.865
throw away as yesterday's news.

00:15:16.865 --> 00:15:19.641
It's something that we should
really stop and think about.

00:15:19.641 --> 00:15:21.240
- What was the one thing
that Forest Whitaker

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:23.401
said to you as you were shooting,

00:15:23.401 --> 00:15:25.908
that really stuck you, that was something?

00:15:25.908 --> 00:15:28.589
- Forest is one of those people

00:15:28.589 --> 00:15:30.933
who he doesn't talk a whole lot.

00:15:30.933 --> 00:15:34.810
Everything he says is really
important and really moving.

00:15:34.810 --> 00:15:37.405
I think that the number one thing

00:15:37.405 --> 00:15:40.572
he told me was to always make the best

00:15:42.639 --> 00:15:44.914
decision that you can make in the moment.

00:15:44.914 --> 00:15:47.921
That's all you can ever do.

00:15:47.921 --> 00:15:51.275
I think he was referring to the pressure

00:15:51.275 --> 00:15:52.727
that surrounds what we make in

00:15:52.727 --> 00:15:54.384
the film based on the true story.

00:15:54.384 --> 00:15:58.062
Making a film that's at the
area where it's taken place.

00:15:58.062 --> 00:16:00.858
Making a film where you don't
have a lot of resources.

00:16:00.858 --> 00:16:02.669
Making a film on a short schedule.

00:16:02.669 --> 00:16:04.577
You just want to make the best decisions

00:16:04.577 --> 00:16:07.485
that you can that's
facing you and to move on.

00:16:07.485 --> 00:16:08.988
I love movies, man.

00:16:08.988 --> 00:16:10.988
I watch 'em incessantly.

00:16:12.979 --> 00:16:14.227
- [Interviewer] Does it inform your work

00:16:14.227 --> 00:16:15.633
or do you consider-
- Absolutely.

00:16:15.633 --> 00:16:17.659
No, absolutely it
definitely informs my work.

00:16:17.659 --> 00:16:19.456
I think that it's the best thing

00:16:19.456 --> 00:16:22.956
for a filmmaker to do, is to watch movies.

00:16:24.077 --> 00:16:25.338
I love doing it.

00:16:25.338 --> 00:16:29.901
When I see good movies, it
reminds me of why I do this.

00:16:29.901 --> 00:16:31.702
There's nothing like seeing a great movie

00:16:31.702 --> 00:16:34.295
and being a filmmaker yourself
'cause it's inspiring.

00:16:34.295 --> 00:16:36.644
It's like wow man, I did this.

00:16:36.644 --> 00:16:40.659
This is what the industry
is capable of doing.

00:16:40.659 --> 00:16:42.995
This is the effect that it's
capable of having of people,

00:16:42.995 --> 00:16:44.465
and it makes you want to get back

00:16:44.465 --> 00:16:46.848
into the lab, and get to work.

00:16:46.848 --> 00:16:48.263
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:16:48.263 --> 00:16:51.308
I made this video because
Jaime Martinez asked me to.

00:16:51.308 --> 00:16:52.483
If there's a famous entrepreneur

00:16:52.483 --> 00:16:54.348
that you want me to profile next,

00:16:54.348 --> 00:16:55.667
leave it down in the comments below

00:16:55.667 --> 00:16:56.716
and I'll see what I can do.

00:16:56.716 --> 00:16:57.990
I'd also love to know what did

00:16:57.990 --> 00:17:00.462
Ryan say that had the
biggest impact on you?

00:17:00.462 --> 00:17:01.534
What was the biggest less that

00:17:01.534 --> 00:17:03.505
you learned from watching this video?

00:17:03.505 --> 00:17:04.968
Super curious to find out.

00:17:04.968 --> 00:17:06.342
Please leave in the comments below.

00:17:06.342 --> 00:17:08.079
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:17:08.079 --> 00:17:10.347
Finally, I wanted to give a
quick shout out to Rose Tan.

00:17:10.347 --> 00:17:12.639
Rose, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book.

00:17:12.639 --> 00:17:15.017
It really, really,
really means a lot to me.

00:17:15.017 --> 00:17:16.651
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:17:16.651 --> 00:17:17.951
I believe in you.

00:17:17.951 --> 00:17:20.145
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:17:20.145 --> 00:17:22.543
and whatever your own word is.

00:17:22.543 --> 00:17:23.738
Much love.

00:17:23.738 --> 00:17:24.893
I'll see you soon.

00:17:24.893 --> 00:17:27.495
(whooshing and booming)

